          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 52 and my LDL cholesterol is 165 mg/dL. Does that number alone decide treatment, what risk bands does guidance use, and which modifiable coronary-risk factors does the corpus describe?",
          "answer": "[removed: this system does not give doses or amounts.] Current guidelines state that treatment decisions are mainly based on your estimated 10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk, not solely on the LDL number. The risk is estimated using factors such as age, sex, race, total cholesterol levels, HDL cholesterol, blood pressure, diabetes status, and smoking. There are four risk bands for adults aged 40 to 75 without established disease:\n\n- Low: estimated 10-year risk below 5%\n- Borderline: 5% to below 7.5%\n- Intermediate: 7.5% to below 20%\n- High: 20% or above\n\nAdditionally, risk-enhancing factors can adjust decisions within the borderline and intermediate bands. These include family history of premature cardiovascular disease, chronic kidney disease, and others (source: \"Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation\").\n\nFor modifiable coronary-risk factors, the corpus highlights several key elements: smoking, high blood pressure, raised LDL cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, and an unhealthy diet high in saturated fat and low in vegetables and fiber, among others (source: \"Coronary artery disease\"). \n\nI could not cover specific treatment options or interventions for your LDL cholesterol, as that depends on individual circumstances and clinician recommendations.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
